-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookups resolve against a local embedded SQLite index that each kiosk builds from the active snapshot. No network access is involved at import or at lookup time.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Importing a snapshot runs as a staged pipeline on the kiosk. The active index is replaced only if every stage succeeds:
+
+1. Verify the signature manifest using the bundled public key.
+2. Reject unsupported schema versions.
+3. Read the newline-delimited JSON records, validating that serial numbers are unique.
+4. Build a new SQLite index in a staging location separate from the active index. Each record is stored with its normalized serial key (see Lookup Behavior) in an indexed column, and the snapshot date is stored in the index so a lookup can report it with or without a matching record.
+5. Validate the built index before it is eligible to become active: the stored record count matches the number of records read, and the serial-key index is present.
+6. Activate the staged index by atomically replacing the active index. The previous index remains in place and queryable until the replacement completes.
+
+A failure at any stage leaves the previously active index in place and reports a useful error. No partially built or partially imported index is ever exposed to lookups.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize the query by trimming surrounding whitespace and uppercasing ASCII letters; serial-number punctuation remains significant. The same normalization is applied to each record's serial key when the index is built, so a query matches by equality on the normalized key. Query the active SQLite index for the normalized serial. Return `Found(record, snapshotDate)` when a row matches and `NotFound(snapshotDate)` otherwise; the active snapshot date is returned in both cases.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index built on each kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Keeps every lookup on local files with no service to run and no network route, matching the offline tenets. The accepted cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives. Both rejected options introduced a registry dependency on the lab network or an external service (timeout handling, credentials, recurring cost) to produce a lookup result the certificate interface receives identically either way. |
+| Serial matching | Equality on a stored, indexed normalized serial key | Normalize only at query time and scan records | A precomputed indexed key makes each lookup a single indexed equality match, meeting the 250 ms goal at 90,000 records, and forces import-time and query-time normalization to be the same rule. |
+| Snapshot date availability | Store the snapshot date inside the active index | Keep it in a sidecar file read at lookup time | A NotFound result must still carry the snapshot date (REG-002); storing it in the index keeps it atomic with the records it describes across an index swap. |
+
